 Collection period. Zone-level year distributions differ systematically: Guinea savanna spans 2009-2019 with a 2018 peak, coastal savanna is concentrated in 2016-2018, deciduous forest in 2013-2021, and the transitional zone falls entirely in 2011-2013. Over 2009-2021 Ghana changed first-line therapy and scaled up chemoprevention, so drug-related allele frequencies would be expected to move with time independently of geography. The confounding is not removable by reweighting, because for the transitional zone and the coastal savanna the year ranges do not overlap at all. The same fact makes every resistance frequency in this report a 2009-2021 average rather than a current value, including the pfk13 zeros.
-Sequencing study (new in this version). Pf8 isolates arrive through named partner studies, and in Ghana those studies are almost perfectly nested within zones. Four of the six zone pairs share no study whatsoever - coastal savanna versus Guinea savanna, coastal savanna versus transitional, deciduous forest versus Guinea savanna, and deciduous forest versus transitional - and the first of these is the pair behind the maximum FST and the polyclonality contrast. Each zone is dominated by one study: the transitional zone is 100% one study (1083-PF-GH-CONWAY, 50 of 50), coastal savanna 89% (387 of 435, 1241-PF-GH-ANINAGYEI), Guinea savanna 74% (2,678 of 3,605, 1168-PF-GH-AMENGA-ETEGO-SM) and deciduous forest 48% (391 of 815, 1200-PF-GH-MAIGA-SM). Batch effects in this dataset are demonstrable, not hypothetical: holding zone constant so that only study varies, call rate differs between the six studies within Guinea savanna at p = 2.8 x 10 to the -197. Deciduous forest draws on five studies in all; across the four of them large enough to test (811 of the zone's 815 isolates, the fifth contributing only 4) call rate differs at p = 7.7 x 10 to the -88, and the polyclonal fraction ranges from 0.430 to 0.578 (Kruskal-Wallis p = 0.0019).
